[
  {
    "id": "copd-breathing-exercises-home",
    "title": "Easy Breathing Exercises for COPD at Home",
    "summary": "Simple daily techniques that help your loved one breathe easier, panic less, and stay active at home.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Watching someone you love struggle for breath is frightening. The good news: a few simple exercises, done daily, can make breathing easier and reduce that panic.\n\n## Why breathing exercises help\n\nIn COPD, air gets trapped in the lungs. The right techniques push stale air out and bring fresh air in - without exhausting the patient.\n\nThey also calm the mind. Less panic means less breathlessness.\n\n## 1. Pursed-Lip Breathing\n\nThe most useful one. Use it during breathlessness or climbing stairs.\n\n1. Breathe in slowly through the nose for 2 counts.\n2. Purse the lips like blowing out a candle.\n3. Breathe out gently for 4 counts.\n\n> Out should be longer than in. This keeps airways open.\n\n## 2. Belly (Diaphragm) Breathing\n\n- Sit relaxed or lie down.\n- One hand on the chest, one on the belly.\n- Breathe in so the **belly** rises, not the chest.\n- Breathe out slowly. Belly falls.\n\nDo this 5 minutes, twice a day.\n\n## 3. Gentle Daily Movement\n\nStill bodies get weaker. Encourage:\n\n- Short, slow walks inside the home.\n- Arm raises while seated.\n- Standing up and sitting down a few times.\n\nRest whenever needed. Slow and steady wins.\n\n## When to stop and get help\n\nCall for urgent help if your loved one has:\n\n- Lips or fingertips turning blue.\n- Breathlessness even at rest.\n- Confusion or unusual drowsiness.\n- Chest pain.\n\n## A Delhi reality\n\nOn high-pollution days, keep windows shut, use a clean indoor space, and avoid outdoor walks. Pollution worsens COPD fast.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEach calm breath you help them take is a small victory - and they add up."
  },
  {
    "id": "home-oxygen-therapy-safety",
    "title": "Using Oxygen at Home: A Safety Guide",
    "summary": "If a concentrator or cylinder has come home, here is how to use it safely and confidently.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "When oxygen equipment arrives home, it can feel intimidating. Take a breath - with a few rules, it becomes simple and safe.\n\n## Rule one: oxygen and fire do not mix\n\nOxygen makes things burn faster. This is the most important safety point.\n\n- No smoking anywhere near the patient or device.\n- Keep away from gas stove, diya, agarbatti, candles, matchsticks.\n- No oil-based creams on the nose - use water-based gel.\n\n## Setting up the concentrator\n\n1. Place it in an open spot, away from walls and curtains (it needs air).\n2. Keep it on a stable surface, not on carpet that blocks vents.\n3. Set the flow **exactly** as the doctor advised - never increase it yourself.\n4. Check the tubing is not bent or pressed.\n\n## Caring for the patient\n\n- Clean the nasal prongs daily with mild soap and water.\n- Watch for soreness behind the ears or on the nose; pad gently with soft cloth.\n- Keep the patient's mouth and lips moist with water-based gel.\n\n## Power and backup\n\nDelhi power cuts are real. A concentrator needs electricity.\n\n- Keep a backup cylinder if the doctor recommends one.\n- Know how long the backup lasts.\n- Save your Housepital and emergency numbers on speed dial.\n\n## Red flags - get help fast\n\n- Breathing stays hard even on oxygen.\n- Lips, face, or nails turn blue or grey.\n- Severe headache, confusion, or drowsiness.\n- The device alarms and will not reset.\n\n> Never change the oxygen flow rate on your own. More is not always safer.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYou learned something new and important today - that quiet competence is exactly what your loved one needs."
  },
  {
    "id": "asthma-diet-triggers-india",
    "title": "Asthma: Foods That Help and Triggers to Avoid",
    "summary": "Smart, everyday food choices and home changes that help keep asthma attacks away.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Caring for someone with asthma means thinking about more than just the inhaler. What they eat and breathe at home matters too.\n\n## Foods that support the lungs\n\nNo food cures asthma, but some help reduce inflammation.\n\n- **Colourful fruit and veg** - orange, papaya, gajar, palak, tomato.\n- **Vitamin D sources** - eggs, fortified milk, a little morning sun.\n- **Warm fluids** - water, soups, tulsi or ginger tea ease congestion.\n- **Omega-rich foods** - walnuts, flaxseed (alsi), fish if non-veg.\n\n## Foods that may trigger\n\nTriggers differ for each person. Watch for these common ones:\n\n- Very cold drinks and ice cream.\n- Heavily fried, oily, or fast food.\n- Packaged foods with strong preservatives.\n- Any food they are personally allergic to.\n\n> Keep a small diary. Note what was eaten before an attack. Patterns appear.\n\n## Home triggers beyond food\n\n- **Dust** - wash bedsheets weekly in hot water.\n- **Agarbatti and dhoop smoke** - keep away from the patient.\n- **Cold weather** - cover the nose and mouth with a scarf.\n- **Pollution** - on bad AQI days, keep windows shut.\n\n## Stay hydrated\n\nThin mucus is easier to clear. Encourage water through the day, especially in dry Delhi winters.\n\n## When it is an emergency\n\nUse the prescribed reliever inhaler and seek urgent help if:\n\n- The inhaler is not working.\n- They cannot speak full sentences.\n- Lips or nails turn blue.\n- The chest pulls in sharply with each breath.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nSmall daily choices in your kitchen and home add up to easier breathing - and you are already making them."
  },
  {
    "id": "what-is-copd-explained-simply",
    "title": "What Is COPD? A Simple Explainer for Families",
    "summary": "Plain-language answers about the lung condition your loved one is living with - no jargon.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "If the doctor said COPD and you nodded without fully understanding, you are not alone. Let us make it clear and simple.\n\n## What COPD means\n\nCOPD stands for Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease. In plain words: the airways are narrowed and damaged, so air gets trapped and breathing becomes hard.\n\nIt is long-term, but it **can be managed well** with the right care.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nThe biggest causes in India:\n\n- Smoking (including bidi and hookah).\n- Years of breathing smoke from chulha or wood fires.\n- Heavy air pollution.\n- Repeated lung infections in the past.\n\n## Signs to recognise\n\n- A cough that will not go away.\n- Breathlessness on walking or climbing stairs.\n- Wheezing - a whistling sound while breathing.\n- More mucus or phlegm than usual.\n\nThese often get slowly worse over months and years.\n\n## What a flare-up is\n\nSometimes symptoms suddenly worsen - more cough, more breathlessness, coloured phlegm. This is called an exacerbation, or flare-up.\n\n> Catching a flare-up early and calling the doctor can prevent a hospital stay.\n\n## How it is managed\n\n- Inhalers and medicines, taken exactly as prescribed by your doctor.\n- Breathing exercises.\n- Avoiding smoke and pollution.\n- Staying active within limits.\n- Yearly flu protection, if the doctor advises.\n\n## The emotional side\n\nBreathlessness causes fear and frustration. Your loved one may feel low or dependent. Your calm presence matters as much as the medicine.\n\n## Get urgent help if\n\n- Breathing is very difficult, even at rest.\n- Lips or nails turn blue.\n- They become confused or very drowsy.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nUnderstanding the condition is the first step to controlling it - and you have just taken it."
  },
  {
    "id": "pneumonia-recovery-myths-busted",
    "title": "Post-Pneumonia Recovery: Myths vs Facts",
    "summary": "Old beliefs can slow recovery after pneumonia. Here is what is true and what to ignore.",
    "category": "Pulmo",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "Your loved one is home after pneumonia, and everyone has advice. Let us clear up the myths so recovery stays on track.\n\n## Myth 1: Once the fever goes, they are fully fine.\n\n**Fact:** The lungs take weeks to heal fully. Tiredness and mild cough can linger. Rushing back to full activity too soon can cause a setback.\n\nLet recovery be gradual.\n\n## Myth 2: Cold foods and curd cause pneumonia to return.\n\n**Fact:** Curd and normal foods do not cause pneumonia. It is caused by infection, not by temperature of food. A balanced diet actually speeds healing.\n\n## Myth 3: Bathing will make the chest infection worse.\n\n**Fact:** A warm bath is fine and keeps the patient comfortable and clean. Just keep the room warm and dry them quickly afterwards.\n\n## Myth 4: Stop the antibiotics once they feel better.\n\n**Fact:** This is dangerous. Stopping early lets the infection return stronger. Always finish the full course exactly as prescribed by your doctor.\n\n## Myth 5: Lying in bed all day is the best rest.\n\n**Fact:** Too much lying still allows mucus to settle in the lungs. Gentle sitting up, short walks, and deep breaths help the lungs clear.\n\n> Rest the body, but keep the lungs gently working.\n\n## What actually helps recovery\n\n- Finish all prescribed medicine.\n- Deep-breathing exercises a few times a day.\n- Warm fluids and a protein-rich diet - dal, eggs, paneer, milk.\n- Plenty of sleep and a smoke-free room.\n\n## Watch for warning signs\n\nCall the doctor urgently if you see:\n\n- Fever returning after improving.\n- Breathlessness getting worse.\n- Chest pain or blue lips.\n- Coughing up blood.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nWith patience and the right care, lungs heal well - you are helping them get there safely."
  },
  {
    "id": "stroke-home-recovery-diet",
    "title": "Eating Right After a Stroke: A Home Diet Guide",
    "summary": "Simple, everyday Indian meals that help your loved one recover from a stroke and stay safe while eating.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 4,
    "body": "Caring for someone after a stroke can feel overwhelming, especially at mealtimes. The right food helps healing, controls blood pressure, and lowers the chance of another stroke.\n\n## Why diet matters after a stroke\n\nA stroke often comes with high BP, diabetes, or high cholesterol. Food is one of the strongest tools you have at home to keep these in check.\n\nGood eating also rebuilds strength and supports the brain as it heals.\n\n## What to put on the plate\n\n- **More vegetables and fruit** - palak, lauki, gajar, papaya, apple, orange.\n- **Whole grains** - roti, brown rice, oats, daliya instead of maida.\n- **Good protein** - dal, sprouts, paneer, eggs, curd, soya.\n- **Healthy fats** - a little mustard or olive oil, a few nuts.\n\n## What to limit\n\n- Extra salt, achaar, papad, namkeen - these raise BP.\n- Deep-fried foods, ghee-heavy sweets, full-cream milk.\n- Packaged and processed snacks.\n\n> Cook with less salt for the whole family. It is easier than cooking separately, and good for everyone.\n\n## If swallowing is difficult\n\nMany stroke patients struggle to swallow. This is serious - food can enter the lungs.\n\n1. Keep them sitting upright while eating and for 30 minutes after.\n2. Offer soft foods - khichdi, mashed dal-rice, dalia, curd, banana.\n3. Make liquids thicker if thin water causes coughing.\n4. Feed slowly, small spoonfuls, no rushing.\n\n**Call for urgent help if** they cough or choke badly, turn blue, or food keeps going down the wrong way.\n\n## Easy day plan\n\n- **Morning:** soft daliya or oats with fruit.\n- **Lunch:** soft roti or rice, dal, well-cooked sabzi, curd.\n- **Evening:** fruit or a handful of soaked nuts.\n- **Dinner:** khichdi or vegetable soup with soft rice.\n\nKeep water within reach all day. Dehydration is common and easy to miss.\n\nProgress may be slow. Some days they eat well, some days less. That is normal - keep going gently.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYou are doing one of the kindest things possible - feeding someone back to strength, one meal at a time."
  },
  {
    "id": "dementia-daily-management-home",
    "title": "Managing Dementia at Home: Day-to-Day Help",
    "summary": "Practical routines and calm responses that make daily life easier for a parent with dementia - and for you.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 4,
    "body": "Looking after a parent with dementia tests your patience and your heart every single day. Small changes to the daily routine can reduce confusion and keep them calm.\n\n## What is happening\n\nDementia slowly affects memory, thinking, and mood. Your loved one is not being difficult on purpose - their brain is changing. Remembering this helps you respond with kindness.\n\n## Build a steady routine\n\nSameness brings comfort. Confusion grows when the day feels unpredictable.\n\n- Wake, eat, and sleep at the same times daily.\n- Keep familiar objects in the same place.\n- Do calming activities - tea together, bhajan, a short walk - at fixed times.\n\n## Make the home safe\n\n1. Remove loose rugs and clutter that cause falls.\n2. Lock away medicines, sharp tools, and matchsticks.\n3. Put a name and phone number in their pocket in case they wander.\n4. Use night lights to reduce evening confusion.\n\n## Handle difficult moments\n\nThey may repeat questions, get angry, or accuse family members. Try not to argue or correct harshly.\n\n- Speak slowly, in short sentences.\n- Use their name and a warm tone.\n- Distract gently - offer food, music, or a photo.\n- Step away for a minute if you feel frustrated. That is okay.\n\n> You cannot reason them out of confusion. You can only meet them where they are, with calm.\n\n## Watch for sundowning\n\nMany patients become restless in the late afternoon and evening. Keep that time quiet - dim lights, less noise, a familiar face nearby.\n\n## When to seek help\n\nCall your doctor if you notice:\n\n- Sudden worsening of confusion (this can mean infection).\n- New fever, poor urine, or refusal to eat or drink.\n- Aggression that puts them or others at risk.\n\n## Care for yourself too\n\nCaregiver burnout is real. You cannot pour from an empty cup.\n\n- Share duties with family. Ask for help - it is not weakness.\n- Take short breaks. Even 15 minutes helps.\n- Talk to someone about how you feel.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour patience, even on the hard days, is a quiet form of love they still feel."
  },
  {
    "id": "epilepsy-seizure-myths-busted",
    "title": "Epilepsy Myths That Put Patients at Risk",
    "summary": "Popular beliefs about seizures can do real harm. Here is the truth, so you can respond the right way.",
    "category": "Neuro",
    "read_minutes": 3,
    "body": "If someone in your family has seizures, you have probably heard plenty of advice from relatives and neighbours. Some of it is dangerous. Let us separate myth from fact.\n\n## Myth 1: Put a spoon or shoe in their mouth during a seizure.\n\n**Fact:** Never put anything in the mouth. It can break teeth, block breathing, or hurt you. A person cannot swallow their tongue.\n\nInstead, turn them gently on their side and protect their head with something soft.\n\n## Myth 2: Make them smell a shoe or onion to stop the fit.\n\n**Fact:** This does nothing. A seizure stops on its own, usually within 1-2 minutes. Just keep them safe and wait calmly.\n\n## Myth 3: Epilepsy is caused by black magic or past sins.\n\n**Fact:** Epilepsy is a medical condition. It comes from changes in the brain's electrical activity - not from spirits, curses, or anything the family did wrong.\n\nIt can be controlled well with proper treatment.\n\n## Myth 4: Once seizures stop, you can stop the medicine.\n\n**Fact:** Stopping medicine suddenly is one of the biggest causes of dangerous seizures. The medicine is working *because* they take it daily.\n\nNever stop or change it without the doctor's advice.\n\n## Myth 5: People with epilepsy cannot study, work, or marry.\n\n**Fact:** Most people with epilepsy live full, normal lives - school, jobs, marriage, children. Good treatment makes this possible.\n\n> The goal is not to fear epilepsy, but to manage it calmly and correctly.\n\n## What to actually do during a seizure\n\n1. Stay calm. Note the time.\n2. Move sharp or hard objects away.\n3. Turn them on their side.\n4. Put something soft under the head.\n5. Do **not** hold them down or force the mouth open.\n\n**Call emergency help if:** the seizure lasts more than 5 minutes, one seizure follows another, they do not wake up, or they are injured or struggling to breathe.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nKnowing the right response turns a frightening moment into one you can handle with confidence."
  },
  {
    "id": "knee-replacement-recovery-exercises",
    "title": "Exercises After Knee Replacement Surgery",
    "summary": "Gentle, safe movements that help your loved one walk again after a knee operation.",
    "category": "Ortho",
    "body": "After knee replacement surgery, the thought of moving that leg can scare both patient and family. But gentle movement is exactly what heals it. Let's go step by step.\n\n## Why early movement matters\n\nResting too much makes the joint stiff and muscles weak. Safe exercise reduces pain, prevents clots, and helps them walk sooner.\n\nMove little and often, not hard and rare.\n\n## 1. Ankle pumps\n\nStart these from day one in bed.\n\n- Move the foot up and down, like pressing a pedal.\n- Repeat 10 times, several times a day.\n- This keeps blood flowing and prevents clots.\n\n## 2. Thigh squeezes\n\n- Lying down, tighten the thigh muscle, pressing the knee down.\n- Hold 5 seconds, relax.\n- Repeat 10 times.\n\n## 3. Knee bends (as advised)\n\n- Slowly slide the heel toward the hip, bending the knee gently.\n- Only as far as comfortable.\n- Straighten slowly. Repeat 10 times.\n\n## 4. Assisted walking\n\n- Use the walker or stick exactly as the physiotherapist showed.\n- Short, frequent walks beat long, tiring ones.\n- Keep the path clear of rugs and clutter.\n\n> Some discomfort is normal. Sharp pain is a signal to stop.\n\n## Managing pain and swelling\n\n- Apply an ice pack wrapped in cloth for 15 minutes after exercise.\n- Keep the leg raised on a pillow when resting.\n- Take pain medicine as prescribed by your doctor, especially before physio.\n\n## Call the doctor if you see\n\n- Calf pain, redness, or swelling (possible clot).\n- Fever, or pus or bad smell from the wound.\n- The knee gives way or can't bear weight at all.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEvery small bend and step is progress - you're helping them walk back to their life.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "preventing-falls-elderly-home",
    "title": "Fall-Proofing Your Home for a Loved One",
    "summary": "Most falls happen at home and most are preventable. Here's how to make every room safer.",
    "category": "Ortho",
    "body": "One fall can change everything - a fracture, a hospital stay, lost confidence. The good news: most home falls are preventable with simple changes you can make today.\n\n## Clear the floor\n\nClutter is the biggest hidden danger.\n\n- Remove loose rugs and mats, or tape them down flat.\n- Keep wires, stools, and bags out of walking paths.\n- Wipe up spills and wet floors immediately.\n\n## Make the bathroom safe\n\nBathrooms cause the most falls in Indian homes.\n\n- Install grab bars near the toilet and bathing area.\n- Use a rubber anti-slip mat.\n- A plastic chair for bathing reduces risk hugely.\n- Keep the floor dry; a wet Indian-style bathroom is very slippery.\n\n## Light the way\n\n- Bright lights in every room and passage.\n- A night light from bed to bathroom for night trips.\n- Keep a torch or phone within reach of the bed.\n\n## Right footwear and support\n\n- Non-slip slippers or shoes with grip, even indoors.\n- No loose chappals or walking in socks.\n- Keep the walking stick or walker beside the bed.\n\n## Smart room setup\n\n1. Keep daily items at waist height - no bending or stretching.\n2. Place the bed at a height that's easy to sit and rise from.\n3. Keep a chair in the bathroom and bedroom for resting.\n\n> A few minutes fixing the home today can prevent months in recovery later.\n\n## After any fall\n\nEven if they seem fine, watch closely. Get medical help if there is:\n\n- Severe pain or inability to move a limb.\n- A visibly bent or swollen bone.\n- A head knock with confusion, vomiting, or drowsiness.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nThe safe home you build gives your loved one freedom to move with confidence again.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "fracture-recovery-diet-bones",
    "title": "Foods That Help Broken Bones Heal",
    "summary": "The right diet speeds fracture recovery. Here are everyday Indian foods that rebuild bone.",
    "category": "Ortho",
    "body": "When a loved one is recovering from a fracture, you want to do everything to help them heal faster. Food is a powerful, often forgotten tool.\n\n## Calcium - the bone builder\n\nBones need calcium to rebuild.\n\n- Milk, curd, paneer, buttermilk.\n- Til (sesame seeds), ragi, almonds.\n- Green leafy veg - palak, methi, sarson.\n\n## Vitamin D - the calcium helper\n\nWithout vitamin D, the body can't use calcium well.\n\n- 15-20 minutes of morning sun.\n- Eggs, fortified milk, mushrooms.\n- A supplement only if prescribed by your doctor.\n\n## Protein - the repair material\n\nProtein rebuilds the tissue around the bone.\n\n- Dal, rajma, chana, sprouts.\n- Eggs, paneer, milk.\n- Chicken or fish, if non-veg.\n\n## Vitamin C and zinc\n\nThese help the bone knit together.\n\n- Orange, amla, guava, lemon, tomato.\n- Whole grains, nuts, seeds.\n\n## What to limit\n\n- Too much tea and coffee - they reduce calcium absorption.\n- Excess salt and packaged snacks.\n- Alcohol and smoking, which slow healing.\n\n## Don't forget fluids and fibre\n\nBed rest causes constipation, made worse by pain medicines.\n\n- Plenty of water through the day.\n- Fibre - fruits, vegetables, whole grains, dalia.\n\n> A simple daily plate: dal, roti, sabzi, curd, a fruit, a glass of milk. Healing food is everyday food.\n\n## Tell the doctor if\n\n- The bone area becomes very red, hot, or swollen.\n- There's fever or discharge near a wound.\n- Pain suddenly worsens instead of improving.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nWith good food and patience, bones heal beautifully - and your kitchen is helping every day.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "bedridden-patient-care-techniques",
    "title": "Caring for a Bed-Bound Patient Safely",
    "summary": "Practical daily techniques to prevent bed sores, stiffness, and infection during long bed rest.",
    "category": "Ortho",
    "body": "Caring for someone confined to bed is demanding work. A few good techniques protect them from complications and make your job easier.\n\n## Prevent bed sores\n\nLong pressure on one spot damages skin. This is the biggest risk.\n\n1. Change their position every 2 hours, even at night.\n2. Use soft pillows to cushion the back, hips, heels, and elbows.\n3. Keep the skin clean and dry.\n4. Check daily for redness on the back, hips, and heels.\n\n> A red patch that doesn't fade is an early bed sore. Relieve pressure there immediately.\n\n## Daily hygiene\n\n- A daily sponge bath keeps skin healthy and the patient comfortable.\n- Dry well in skin folds.\n- Change bedsheets and clothes regularly; keep them wrinkle-free.\n- Apply gentle moisturiser to dry skin.\n\n## Keep joints moving\n\nStill limbs become stiff and painful.\n\n- Gently move each arm and leg through its range, a few times daily.\n- Support the limb fully while moving it.\n- Stop if it causes sharp pain.\n\n## Toileting and infection\n\n- Keep the area clean after every toilet use.\n- Watch for signs of urine infection - fever, smelly or cloudy urine.\n- Encourage fluids unless the doctor has limited them.\n\n## Protect the lungs\n\nLying flat can let mucus settle.\n\n- Prop them up with pillows for part of the day.\n- Encourage slow deep breaths a few times a day.\n\n## Get help if you notice\n\n- An open bed sore or broken skin.\n- Fever, cough, or breathlessness.\n- A swollen, painful calf (possible clot).\n- Refusal to eat or drink.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nThe steady, careful attention you give protects them in ways they may never see - but it matters enormously.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "ortho-recovery-myths-busted",
    "title": "Bone and Joint Recovery: Myths vs Facts",
    "summary": "Common beliefs about fractures and joint surgery can slow healing. Here's the truth.",
    "category": "Ortho",
    "body": "When a bone breaks or a joint is replaced, advice pours in from every relative. Some of it can actually harm recovery. Let's separate myth from fact.\n\n## Myth 1: Complete bed rest is best for healing.\n\n**Fact:** Too much rest causes stiffness, weakness, and even clots. Safe, guided movement helps most fractures and joints heal faster. Follow the physiotherapist's plan.\n\n## Myth 2: Drinking lime water or eating cement-like foods joins the bone.\n\n**Fact:** No single food magically joins bone. A balanced diet rich in calcium, protein, and vitamin D supports healing - there's no shortcut food.\n\n## Myth 3: Tying the limb tightly helps it set.\n\n**Fact:** Tight wrapping can cut off blood flow and cause serious damage. Only use the support exactly as the doctor or cast technician advised.\n\n## Myth 4: Massaging the fracture area speeds recovery.\n\n**Fact:** Massaging a fresh fracture can move the bone and harm healing. Never massage the injury unless a professional advises it.\n\n## Myth 5: Once the cast is off, everything is normal again.\n\n**Fact:** After a cast comes off, the muscles are weak and the joint stiff. Recovery exercises are needed for weeks to regain full strength.\n\n## Myth 6: Pain means you should stop moving completely.\n\n**Fact:** Mild discomfort during prescribed exercise is normal. Stopping all movement causes stiffness. Sharp pain, though, is a signal to pause and check with the doctor.\n\n> Healing is active, not passive. The right movement at the right time is medicine.\n\n## What truly helps\n\n- Follow the physiotherapy plan.\n- Eat for bone strength.\n- Keep the wound clean and dry.\n- Attend follow-up appointments.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nKnowing the facts means you can guide recovery with confidence instead of fear.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "elderly-nutrition-low-appetite",
    "title": "Feeding Elderly Parents Who Eat Too Little",
    "summary": "When an ageing parent loses appetite, here's how to keep them nourished and strong.",
    "category": "Elderly Care",
    "body": "It worries you to see your parent push food around the plate and eat just a few bites. Appetite naturally drops with age, but a few simple changes can keep them well-fed.\n\n## Why appetite drops with age\n\n- Weaker sense of taste and smell.\n- Less activity, so less hunger.\n- Dental problems making chewing hard.\n- Some medicines reduce appetite.\n\nUnderstanding the cause helps you respond gently.\n\n## Small, frequent meals\n\nLarge plates overwhelm them.\n\n- Offer small portions every 2-3 hours.\n- Make each bite count - add ghee, nuts, or paneer for energy.\n- Keep healthy snacks handy - banana, curd, roasted chana.\n\n## Make food easy to chew\n\n- Soft khichdi, dalia, mashed dal-rice, idli, upma.\n- Well-cooked, soft sabzi.\n- Soak roti in dal or milk if chewing is hard.\n- Check if dentures fit well.\n\n## Watch hydration\n\nElderly people often don't feel thirsty, so they drink too little.\n\n- Offer water, nimbu-pani, buttermilk, soups through the day.\n- Don't wait for them to ask.\n- Dark urine is a sign they need more fluids.\n\n## Make meals pleasant\n\n- Eat together; company encourages eating.\n- Serve favourite foods and familiar tastes.\n- Don't scold or force. Pressure kills appetite.\n\n> A calm, shared meal nourishes more than a forced one ever will.\n\n## Speak to the doctor if\n\n- They stop eating or drinking for a day.\n- There's rapid, unexplained weight loss.\n- Swallowing becomes painful or causes coughing.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEach nourishing meal you patiently offer keeps your parent stronger - and your care is felt in every bite.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "elderly-loneliness-mental-health",
    "title": "Easing Loneliness in Ageing Parents",
    "summary": "Loneliness quietly harms elderly health. Here's how to lift their mood and keep them connected.",
    "category": "Elderly Care",
    "body": "You may notice your parent has gone quiet, sleeps more, or seems low. Loneliness in old age is common and real - and it affects both mind and body.\n\n## Why it happens\n\n- Friends and a spouse may have passed away.\n- Children are busy with work and their own families.\n- Reduced mobility limits going out.\n- Loss of a sense of purpose after retirement.\n\nLoneliness isn't weakness. It deserves gentle attention.\n\n## Stay connected daily\n\n- Sit and talk, even 15 minutes, without your phone.\n- Ask about their past - they love to share memories.\n- A video call with grandchildren brightens the whole day.\n\n## Give them purpose\n\nFeeling useful restores dignity.\n\n- Involve them in small decisions and family matters.\n- Let them help - folding clothes, advising on cooking, telling stories to kids.\n- Encourage a hobby - gardening, reading, bhajan, knitting.\n\n## Encourage gentle activity\n\n- A short walk in the park, meeting neighbours.\n- Joining a satsang, temple group, or senior club.\n- Light morning yoga or stretching.\n\nActivity lifts mood naturally.\n\n## Watch mood and sleep\n\nLow mood can deepen into depression. Notice if they:\n\n- Lose interest in everything.\n- Sleep too much or too little.\n- Speak of being a burden or having no reason to live.\n\n> Comments like 'I'm just a burden' are a cry for connection, not just words. Take them seriously.\n\n## Seek help if\n\n- Sadness lasts for weeks and won't lift.\n- They stop eating, bathing, or caring for themselves.\n- They talk about wanting to die or harming themselves - get professional help promptly.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour time and warmth are the best medicine for a lonely heart - and you give them freely.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "elderly-mobility-daily-exercises",
    "title": "Gentle Daily Exercises for Seniors",
    "summary": "Simple seated and standing movements that keep elderly parents mobile, balanced, and independent.",
    "category": "Elderly Care",
    "body": "Helping your ageing parent stay mobile is one of the greatest gifts - it keeps them independent and confident. These gentle exercises can be done right at home.\n\n## Why movement matters\n\nWith age, muscles weaken and joints stiffen. Daily gentle exercise keeps them strong, improves balance, and prevents falls.\n\nA little every day beats a lot once in a while.\n\n## Seated exercises (great for the weak or frail)\n\n1. **Marching:** Sitting in a chair, lift each knee up and down, like marching. 10 times each leg.\n2. **Arm raises:** Raise both arms forward and up, then lower slowly. 10 times.\n3. **Ankle circles:** Rotate each foot in circles. Keeps blood flowing.\n4. **Shoulder rolls:** Roll the shoulders backward and forward. Eases stiffness.\n\n## Standing exercises (with support)\n\n- **Sit-to-stand:** Stand up and sit down from a chair slowly, a few times. Builds leg strength.\n- **Heel raises:** Hold a chair, rise onto the toes, lower down. 10 times.\n- **Side leg lifts:** Holding support, lift one leg sideways. Improves balance.\n\n## Daily walking\n\n- Short walks inside the home or in the park.\n- Use a stick or walker if needed.\n- Indoors on high-pollution or very hot days.\n\n## Safety first\n\n- Always have support nearby - a chair, wall, or your hand.\n- Wear non-slip footwear.\n- Stop if there's chest pain, dizziness, or breathlessness.\n\n> Slow and steady, every day. Consistency keeps them moving for years.\n\n## Check with the doctor if\n\n- There's new joint pain or swelling.\n- They feel dizzy or breathless during activity.\n- Balance has suddenly worsened.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEvery gentle movement you encourage today helps your parent stay independent tomorrow.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "elderly-medication-management-home",
    "title": "Managing Multiple Medicines for Elders",
    "summary": "Juggling many pills is confusing and risky. Here's how to keep your elder's medicines safe and on time.",
    "category": "Elderly Care",
    "body": "If your elderly parent takes several medicines a day, keeping track can feel like a full-time job. Mistakes are easy - and risky. Here's how to stay organised.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nElders often take pills for BP, sugar, heart, joints, and more. Wrong doses or missed pills can cause real harm. A little system prevents big problems.\n\n## Use a pill organiser\n\n- Buy a weekly pill box with day and time sections.\n- Fill it once a week, calmly, at a fixed time.\n- One look tells you if a dose was missed.\n\n## Keep a medicine list\n\nMaintain one updated list with:\n\n1. Name of each medicine.\n2. Dose and timing.\n3. Whether before or after food.\n4. What it's for.\n\nKeep a copy in your phone and carry it to every doctor visit.\n\n## Build a routine\n\n- Link pills to daily habits - morning tea, lunch, bedtime.\n- Set phone alarms for each dose.\n- Give medicines with the right food rule (before/after meals).\n\n## Avoid common mistakes\n\n- **Never** stop or change a dose without asking the doctor - even BP or sugar medicines that seem fine to skip.\n- Don't mix new home remedies or supplements without checking; they can clash with medicines.\n- Check expiry dates.\n- Store medicines in a cool, dry place, away from children.\n\n> When in doubt about any medicine, ask the doctor or pharmacist. Never guess.\n\n## At every refill and visit\n\n- Reconcile the list - has anything been added or stopped?\n- Ask if any medicine can now be reduced.\n- Report side effects you've noticed.\n\n## Call the doctor if\n\n- A dose is doubled by mistake.\n- New symptoms appear after starting a medicine.\n- They vomit soon after taking pills.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour careful tracking keeps your parent safe every single day - quiet work that truly protects them.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "elderly-care-myths-busted",
    "title": "Old-Age Health Myths to Stop Believing",
    "summary": "Common beliefs about ageing can lead to poor care. Here are the facts every family should know.",
    "category": "Elderly Care",
    "body": "When caring for ageing parents, we often follow advice passed down for generations. Some of it is wrong and can harm their health. Let's set it straight.\n\n## Myth 1: BP ki dawai zindagi bhar nahi leni chahiye.\n\n**Fact:** Most BP medicines are meant to be taken long-term. Stopping because the reading is normal is dangerous - the reading is normal *because* of the medicine. Only the doctor can change it.\n\n## Myth 2: Old people should rest all day and avoid exertion.\n\n**Fact:** Too much rest weakens muscles and bones and raises fall risk. Gentle daily activity keeps elders stronger, sharper, and more independent.\n\n## Myth 3: Forgetfulness is just normal old age, nothing to check.\n\n**Fact:** Mild forgetfulness can be normal, but sudden or worsening memory loss, confusion, or personality change should be checked. Some causes are treatable.\n\n## Myth 4: Elderly people don't need much water.\n\n**Fact:** Elders feel less thirst but still need fluids. Too little water causes weakness, confusion, and urine infections. Offer fluids regularly.\n\n## Myth 5: Aches and low mood are just part of getting old - accept them.\n\n**Fact:** Constant pain and lasting sadness are not just age. Both can be treated. Your parent doesn't have to suffer silently.\n\n## Myth 6: Sugar and BP have no symptoms in elders, so no need to monitor.\n\n**Fact:** These conditions often stay silent yet cause harm. Regular monitoring, as advised, catches problems early.\n\n> Loving care means questioning old beliefs, not just following them.\n\n## What truly helps\n\n- Take chronic medicines exactly as prescribed.\n- Keep them gently active and well-hydrated.\n- Take new symptoms seriously, including mood.\n- Attend regular check-ups.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nReplacing myths with facts is one of the best ways to protect the parents who once protected you.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "postpartum-recovery-japa-diet",
    "title": "Nourishing Food for the New Mother (Japa)",
    "summary": "Gentle, traditional, and balanced Indian foods that help a new mother heal and feed her baby well.",
    "category": "Mother and Baby Care",
    "body": "Welcome to these precious, tiring early weeks. As a new mother recovers, the right food helps her heal, build strength, and make good milk for the baby. Let's nourish her gently.\n\n## Why japa nutrition matters\n\nAfter delivery, the body has worked hard and lost blood and energy. Warm, nourishing meals rebuild strength and support breastfeeding.\n\nFood should be easy to digest and full of love.\n\n## What to include daily\n\n- **Whole grains** - soft roti, rice, dalia, oats for energy.\n- **Protein** - dal, paneer, eggs, milk, soaked almonds.\n- **Iron-rich foods** - palak, beetroot, dates, jaggery (gud).\n- **Healthy fats** - ghee in moderation, helps healing.\n- **Traditional aids** - ajwain water, gond ke laddu, methi - comforting and time-tested.\n\n## Fluids for milk and recovery\n\n- Plenty of water through the day.\n- Warm milk, soups, dal, coconut water.\n- Saunf or jeera water can ease digestion.\n\n> A well-hydrated mother makes milk more easily. Keep a glass of water by her feeding spot.\n\n## Eat warm, eat often\n\n- Small, frequent, warm meals digest best.\n- Avoid very cold, heavy, or deep-fried foods early on.\n- Don't skip meals during night feeds - keep a snack ready.\n\n## A note on rest\n\nFood heals best alongside rest. Let her sleep when the baby sleeps. Family can take over so she eats and rests in peace.\n\n## Speak to the doctor if\n\n- She has heavy bleeding, high fever, or severe pain.\n- She feels very weak, dizzy, or breathless.\n- She has no appetite for days.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nWith warm food, rest, and gentle care, a new mother blooms back to strength - and you're a part of that healing.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "newborn-care-basics-first-weeks",
    "title": "Newborn Care: The Gentle First Weeks",
    "summary": "Simple, reassuring guidance on holding, feeding, sleeping, and soothing your new baby at home.",
    "category": "Mother and Baby Care",
    "body": "Holding a brand-new baby can feel wonderful and a little frightening at once. Take a breath - you're learning, and that's exactly right. Here are the gentle basics.\n\n## Feeding\n\n- Newborns feed often - every 2-3 hours, day and night.\n- Feed on demand, whenever baby shows hunger signs (rooting, sucking hands).\n- After feeding, hold baby upright and gently pat the back to burp.\n\n## Holding and handling\n\n- Always support the head and neck - they can't yet.\n- Wash hands before handling the baby.\n- Hold skin-to-skin often; it calms baby and helps bonding.\n\n## Safe sleep\n\n- Lay baby on the **back** to sleep, never on the tummy.\n- A firm, flat surface, no soft pillows or loose blankets near the face.\n- Keep the room comfortably warm, not too hot.\n\n## Bathing and cord care\n\n- A sponge bath is enough until the cord stump falls off.\n- Keep the cord area clean and dry; let it heal naturally.\n- Don't apply oils, powder, or anything to the cord unless advised.\n\n## Diaper and skin\n\n- Change diapers often to prevent rash.\n- Clean gently, pat dry, allow some open-air time.\n- Baby's skin is delicate - use mild products only.\n\n## Soothing a crying baby\n\nCrying is baby's only language. Check for:\n\n1. Hunger.\n2. Wet or dirty diaper.\n3. Too hot or too cold.\n4. Need to burp, or just wanting to be held.\n\n> Gentle rocking, a soft voice, and your warmth soothe more than anything.\n\n## Call the doctor urgently if baby\n\n- Has fever, or feels cold and floppy.\n- Refuses to feed or feeds very poorly.\n- Has fast or difficult breathing, or turns blue.\n- Has very few wet diapers or yellow skin/eyes that worsen.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYou and your baby are learning each other, day by day - and you're doing beautifully.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "breastfeeding-help-new-mothers",
    "title": "Breastfeeding Made Easier: A Gentle Guide",
    "summary": "Practical, kind support for new mothers learning to feed - latch, supply, and common worries.",
    "category": "Mother and Baby Care",
    "body": "Breastfeeding is natural, but it doesn't always come naturally at first. If you're tired and unsure, that's normal. A little guidance makes it easier for both mother and baby.\n\n## Getting the latch right\n\nA good latch is the key to comfortable feeding.\n\n- Hold baby's whole body facing you, tummy to tummy.\n- Bring baby to the breast, not breast to baby.\n- Baby's mouth should cover much of the dark area, not just the tip.\n- It shouldn't hurt. Pain usually means the latch needs adjusting.\n\n## Building milk supply\n\nThe more baby feeds, the more milk the body makes.\n\n- Feed often, on demand - every 2-3 hours.\n- Empty one side well before switching.\n- Stay hydrated and eat enough; skipping meals lowers energy.\n- Rest as much as possible. Stress and exhaustion affect milk.\n\n> Milk works on demand and supply. Frequent feeding tells the body to make more.\n\n## Common worries\n\n- **Is baby getting enough?** Good signs: 6+ wet diapers a day, steady weight gain, baby settles after feeds.\n- **First milk (colostrum)** is thick and yellow - it's precious, full of protection. Don't discard it.\n- **Sore nipples** - fix the latch; let a little milk air-dry on them.\n\n## Caring for the mother\n\n- Set up a comfortable feeding corner with water and a snack.\n- Let her feed in peace; family can handle other chores.\n- Encourage rest between feeds.\n\n## Seek help if\n\n- The breast becomes red, hot, hard, and painful with fever (possible infection).\n- Baby isn't feeding or is very sleepy and floppy.\n- There are very few wet diapers.\n- Feeding stays painful despite latch changes - ask for lactation support.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEvery feed is a quiet act of love. Be patient with yourself - you and your baby are learning together.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "postpartum-emotional-wellbeing-baby-blues",
    "title": "A New Mother's Mind: Caring for Her Heart",
    "summary": "Tears, worry, and exhaustion after birth are common. Here's how to support a new mother's mental health.",
    "category": "Mother and Baby Care",
    "body": "Bringing home a baby is joyful - and also overwhelming, exhausting, and emotional. If the new mother is crying, anxious, or low, she is not failing. She needs gentle support.\n\n## Baby blues are common\n\nIn the first days after birth, many mothers feel:\n\n- Sudden tears for no clear reason.\n- Mood swings, irritability.\n- Worry and tiredness.\n\nThis is very common and usually eases within two weeks as the body and hormones settle.\n\n## How family can help\n\nThe single biggest support is sharing the load.\n\n- Take over chores so she can rest and feed.\n- Let her sleep when the baby sleeps.\n- Listen without judging or dismissing her feelings.\n- Avoid criticism about feeding or the baby's care.\n\n> 'You're doing well' matters more than any advice right now.\n\n## Small things that lift the mood\n\n- A warm meal she didn't have to cook.\n- A few minutes to bathe in peace.\n- Sunlight and fresh air.\n- Gentle company, not a crowd of visitors.\n\n## When it's more than baby blues\n\nSometimes low mood deepens into postpartum depression. Watch for, beyond two weeks:\n\n- Deep sadness or emptiness that won't lift.\n- No interest in the baby or in herself.\n- Severe anxiety, sleeplessness even when baby sleeps.\n- Feeling hopeless or like a bad mother.\n\nThis is a medical condition, not weakness - and it is treatable.\n\n## Get help promptly if she\n\n- Talks about harming herself or the baby.\n- Cannot care for herself or the baby.\n- Seems disconnected from reality.\n\nReach a doctor or mental-health professional without delay. There is no shame in it.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nA cared-for mother can care for her baby. Holding her heart gently is one of the kindest things you can do.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "postpartum-myths-busted",
    "title": "Post-Delivery Myths Every Family Should Know",
    "summary": "Old japa rules can confuse new mothers. Here are common myths, gently corrected with facts.",
    "category": "Mother and Baby Care",
    "body": "After delivery, every elder shares a rule - what to eat, what to avoid, what's forbidden. Some traditions comfort; some can harm. Let's gently separate myth from fact.\n\n## Myth 1: Delivery ke baad 40 din paani nahi peena.\n\n**Fact:** A new mother needs plenty of fluids - for healing and to make milk. Restricting water can cause dehydration and lower milk supply. Warm water and fluids are good and necessary.\n\n## Myth 2: Don't bathe for many days after delivery.\n\n**Fact:** Gentle bathing keeps the mother clean and prevents infection. Warm water in a warm room is fine. Cleanliness supports healing, especially around stitches.\n\n## Myth 3: Colostrum (the first yellow milk) is dirty - throw it away.\n\n**Fact:** Colostrum is the baby's first and most powerful protection, rich in nutrients and immunity. It should be the baby's very first feed, never discarded.\n\n## Myth 4: Tight belly binding pushes the tummy back fast.\n\n**Fact:** Very tight binding can press internal organs and slow healing, especially after a C-section. Gentle support is fine; tight wrapping is not. Ask your doctor.\n\n## Myth 5: A new mother must stay still in bed for weeks.\n\n**Fact:** Rest is important, but complete stillness raises the risk of blood clots. Gentle movement and short walks (as advised) aid recovery.\n\n## Myth 6: If baby cries, the mother's milk isn't enough - start outside milk.\n\n**Fact:** Babies cry for many reasons. Frequent feeding builds supply. Starting other milk too soon, without advice, can reduce breastfeeding success.\n\n> Honour the love behind traditions, but let the mother's health and the doctor's advice lead.\n\n## What truly helps\n\n- Plenty of fluids and warm, nourishing food.\n- Gentle hygiene and rest.\n- Early, frequent breastfeeding, including colostrum.\n- Following medical advice over fixed customs.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nChoosing facts with love protects both mother and baby in these tender weeks.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "discharge-wound-care-home",
    "title": "Caring for a Surgical Wound at Home",
    "summary": "Simple, safe steps to keep your loved one's wound clean and healing well after discharge.",
    "category": "Post-hospitalisation Care",
    "body": "Bringing a loved one home after surgery brings relief - and the new worry of caring for their wound. With a few clear steps, you can keep it clean and healing well.\n\n## Keep it clean and dry\n\nThis is the golden rule of wound care.\n\n- Always wash your hands before and after touching the area.\n- Keep the dressing dry; protect it during bathing.\n- Change the dressing exactly as the hospital advised, no more, no less.\n- Don't apply any home powder, oil, or paste on the wound.\n\n## Check the wound daily\n\nLook for healthy healing versus warning signs.\n\n- **Healthy:** edges coming together, mild redness fading, less pain each day.\n- **Concerning:** spreading redness, swelling, warmth, pus, or bad smell.\n\n## Changing a dressing safely\n\n1. Wash hands; lay out clean supplies.\n2. Gently remove the old dressing.\n3. Clean as instructed, only with what the doctor advised.\n4. Apply the fresh dressing without touching the inner pad.\n5. Dispose of old materials safely.\n\n> When unsure, it's always safe to leave a dressing alone and call your care team.\n\n## Support healing from inside\n\n- Protein helps wounds close - dal, eggs, paneer, milk.\n- Vitamin C - orange, amla, guava.\n- Plenty of fluids.\n\n## Stay on medicines\n\nFinish all prescribed medicines, especially antibiotics, exactly as your doctor directed - even if they feel better.\n\n## Call the doctor urgently if\n\n- The wound has pus, a bad smell, or spreading redness.\n- There's fever or rising pain.\n- The wound opens up or bleeds.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour careful, clean attention is exactly what a healing wound needs - you've got this.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "preventing-hospital-readmission",
    "title": "Avoiding a Return Trip to the Hospital",
    "summary": "Most re-admissions are preventable. Here's how to keep your loved one safely on the path to recovery.",
    "category": "Post-hospitalisation Care",
    "body": "After a hospital stay, the last thing anyone wants is to go back. The good news: many re-admissions can be prevented with attentive home care. Here's how.\n\n## Understand the discharge plan\n\nBefore leaving, or right after, be clear on:\n\n- What condition was treated and what to watch for.\n- Every medicine - name, dose, timing.\n- Diet and activity instructions.\n- The date of the follow-up appointment.\n\nAsk the team to explain anything unclear. There are no silly questions.\n\n## Get medicines exactly right\n\nMedicine errors are a top cause of re-admission.\n\n- Use a pill organiser and a written list.\n- Set alarms for each dose.\n- Never skip or stop, even if they feel better, without asking the doctor.\n\n## Support recovery at home\n\n- Nourishing, protein-rich meals to rebuild strength.\n- Plenty of fluids.\n- Gentle activity as advised - too much rest causes its own problems.\n\n## Don't miss follow-ups\n\nFollow-up visits catch small problems before they become emergencies. Keep every appointment, even if your loved one feels fine.\n\n## Watch for early warning signs\n\nCatching trouble early keeps them home. Watch for:\n\n- Fever or chills.\n- Worsening pain, breathlessness, or swelling.\n- Confusion, dizziness, or extreme weakness.\n- Not eating, drinking, or passing urine normally.\n\n> A small problem noticed today often prevents an emergency tomorrow.\n\n## Seek urgent help if\n\n- There's chest pain or severe breathlessness.\n- High fever that won't come down.\n- Sudden confusion, fainting, or a wound turning bad.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour watchful, steady care is the strongest protection against a return trip - and it's working.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "post-discharge-medication-adherence",
    "title": "Keeping Recovery Medicines on Track",
    "summary": "After discharge, taking medicines correctly is critical. Here's how to stay organised and safe.",
    "category": "Post-hospitalisation Care",
    "body": "Coming home with a bag full of new medicines is confusing for any family. Getting them right is one of the most important things you can do for recovery.\n\n## Start with a clear list\n\nMake one master list showing:\n\n1. Each medicine's name.\n2. Dose and how many times a day.\n3. Before or after food.\n4. What it's for and how long to continue.\n\nKeep it on your phone and on the fridge.\n\n## Organise the doses\n\n- Use a weekly pill box with day and time slots.\n- Fill it once a week, calmly.\n- One glance shows whether a dose was taken.\n\n## Build it into the day\n\n- Link doses to meals or daily routines.\n- Set phone alarms.\n- Keep water and the pill box where they sit most.\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Stopping antibiotics early** because they feel better - finish the full course as prescribed by your doctor.\n- **Doubling up** after a missed dose - instead, ask the doctor or pharmacist what to do.\n- **Mixing in old medicines or home remedies** that may clash with new ones.\n\n> Never adjust a dose on your own guesswork. One quick call to the doctor keeps it safe.\n\n## At the follow-up visit\n\n- Carry the full list and the medicine boxes.\n- Ask which medicines continue, stop, or change.\n- Report any side effects you've seen.\n\n## Call the doctor if\n\n- A medicine causes rash, vomiting, swelling, or breathlessness.\n- A dose is taken twice by mistake.\n- They can't keep medicines down due to vomiting.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nYour careful tracking turns a confusing pile of pills into a safe, steady path to recovery.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "post-discharge-recovery-diet",
    "title": "Eating to Recover After a Hospital Stay",
    "summary": "The right food rebuilds strength after illness or surgery. Here are simple Indian recovery meals.",
    "category": "Post-hospitalisation Care",
    "body": "After a hospital stay, your loved one is often weak and has little appetite. The right food, given gently, rebuilds their strength faster than anything else.\n\n## Protein rebuilds the body\n\nAfter illness or surgery, the body needs extra protein to repair.\n\n- Dal, rajma, chana, sprouts.\n- Eggs, paneer, milk, curd.\n- Soft chicken or fish, if non-veg.\n\nInclude some protein in every meal.\n\n## Start light, build up\n\nIf the stomach is weak, begin gentle.\n\n- Khichdi, dalia, curd-rice, soft idli, vegetable soup.\n- As strength returns, add roti, sabzi, and more variety.\n- Small, frequent meals digest better than large ones.\n\n## Vitamins for healing\n\n- Vitamin C - orange, amla, guava, lemon - helps wounds heal.\n- Iron - palak, beetroot, dates - rebuilds blood and energy.\n- Colourful fruits and vegetables daily.\n\n## Hydration is vital\n\n- Water, nimbu-pani, coconut water, buttermilk, soups.\n- Especially important if there was fever, vomiting, or loose motions.\n\n## Prevent constipation\n\nBed rest and medicines often cause constipation.\n\n- Fibre - fruits, vegetables, whole grains.\n- Plenty of fluids.\n- Gentle movement as allowed.\n\n## Go easy on\n\n- Oily, deep-fried, and very spicy food early on.\n- Outside and packaged food while immunity is low.\n- Too much tea and coffee.\n\n> A simple recovery plate: dal, soft rice or roti, well-cooked sabzi, curd, a fruit. Healing food is everyday food.\n\n## Tell the doctor if\n\n- They can't eat or drink for a day.\n- There's vomiting, severe diarrhoea, or rapid weight loss.\n- Swallowing becomes difficult or painful.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nEach nourishing meal you offer is rebuilding their strength - recovery is happening, bite by bite.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  },
  {
    "id": "post-hospitalisation-myths-busted",
    "title": "Recovery Myths After a Hospital Stay",
    "summary": "Well-meaning advice can slow healing after discharge. Here are common myths, set straight.",
    "category": "Post-hospitalisation Care",
    "body": "When a loved one returns from hospital, advice flows from every relative. Some of it can quietly slow recovery. Let's separate myth from fact so you can care with confidence.\n\n## Myth 1: Complete bed rest gives the fastest recovery.\n\n**Fact:** Too much lying still causes weakness, stiffness, clots, and chest problems. Gentle movement, as advised, helps most patients recover faster and safer.\n\n## Myth 2: Stop the medicines once they feel better.\n\n**Fact:** Feeling better doesn't mean fully healed. Stopping early - especially antibiotics - lets illness return stronger. Finish every course as prescribed by your doctor.\n\n## Myth 3: Fever mein nahaana mana hai - don't bathe during illness.\n\n**Fact:** Gentle bathing or sponging keeps the patient clean and comfortable and prevents infection. Just keep the room warm and dry them well afterwards.\n\n## Myth 4: Avoid all protein and heaty foods after surgery.\n\n**Fact:** Protein is exactly what the body needs to repair. Dal, eggs, paneer, and milk speed healing. Avoiding them slows recovery.\n\n## Myth 5: Follow-up visits aren't needed if the patient feels fine.\n\n**Fact:** Follow-ups catch hidden problems early - before they become emergencies. Keep every appointment, even when all seems well.\n\n## Myth 6: Home remedies can replace the prescribed treatment.\n\n**Fact:** Home remedies may comfort, but they don't replace medicines. Some even clash with them. Always check before adding anything new.\n\n> The kindest care follows facts and the doctor's plan, not just old habits.\n\n## What truly helps\n\n- Gentle, gradual activity.\n- Finishing all medicines.\n- Good hygiene and nourishing, protein-rich food.\n- Attending follow-ups and watching for warning signs.\n\n*This is general guidance. For your specific situation, talk to your doctor or your Housepital Health Manager.*\n\nReplacing myths with facts keeps your loved one safely on the path home - and you're guiding them well.",
    "read_minutes": 3
  }
]
''';
